    |\nL211: 【104†LICENSE】  | 【104†LICENSE】  |    |\nL212: 【105†McPaw.gif】  | 【105†McPaw.gif】  |    |\nL213: 【106†README.md】  | 【106†README.md】  |    |\nL214: 【107†go.mod】  | 【107†go.mod】  |    |\nL215: 【108†go.sum】  | 【108†go.sum】  |    |\nL216: 【109†package.json】  | 【109†package.json】  |    |\nL217: [Button: View all files]\nL218: ## Repository files navigation\nL219: \nL220:   *   * 【110†README】\nL221:   * 【110†MIT license】\nL222: \nL223: More items\nL224: \nL225: # ICICLE\nL226: \nL227: ICICLE is a high-performance cryptographic acceleration library designed to optimize cryptographic computations across various hardware platforms, including CPUs, GPUs, and other accelerators.\nL228: ## Background\nL229: \nL230: Zero Knowledge Proofs (ZKPs) are considered one of the greatest achievements of modern cryptography. Accordingly, ZKPs are expected to disrupt a number of industries and will usher in an era of trustless and privacy preserving services and infrastructure.\nL231: \nL232: We believe that ICICLE will be a cornerstone in the acceleration of ZKPs:\nL233:   * Versatility: Supports multiple hardware platforms, making it adaptable to various computational environments.\nL234:   * Efficiency: Designed to leverage the parallel nature of ZK computations, whether on GPUs, CPUs, or other accelerators.\nL235:   * Scalability: Provides an easy-to-use and scalable solution for developers, allowing them to optimize cryptographic operations with minimal effort.\nL236: ## Getting Started\nL237: \nL238: This guide will help you get started with ICICLE in C++, Rust, and Go.\nL239: \nL240: Note\nL241: \nL242: Developers: We highly recommend reading our 【71†documentation†dev.ingonyama.com】 for a comprehensive explanation of ICICLE’s capabilities.\nL243: \nL244: Tip\nL245: \nL246: Try out ICICLE by running some 【111†examples】 available in C++, Rust, and Go bindings. Check out our install-and-use examples in 【112†C++】, 【113†Rust】 and 【114†Go】\nL247: ### Prerequisites\nL248: \nL249:   * Any compatible hardware: ICICLE supports various hardware, including CPUs, Nvidia GPUs, and other accelerators.\nL250:   * 【115†CMake†cmake.org】, Version 3.18 or above. Latest version recommended. Required only if building from source.\nL251:   * 【116†CUDA Toolkit†developer.nvidia.com】, Required only if using NVIDIA GPUs (version 12.0 or newer).\nL252: \nL253: Note\nL254: \nL255: For older GPUs that only support CUDA 11, ICICLE may still function, but official support is for CUDA 12 onwards.\nL256: ### Accessing Hardware\nL257: \nL258: If you don't have access to an Nvidia GPU we have some options for you.\nL259: \nL260: 【117†Google Colab†colab.google】 offers a free 【118†T4 GPU†www.nvidia.com】 instance and ICICLE can be used with it, reference this guide for setting up your 【119†Google Colab workplace†dev.ingonyama.com】.\nL261: \nL262: If you require more compute and have an interesting research project, we have 【120†bounty and grant programs†medium.com】.\nL263: ## Building ICICLE from source\nL264: \nL265: ICICLE provides build systems for C++, Rust, and Go. Each build system incorporates the core ICICLE library, which contains the essential cryptographic primitives. Refer to the 【121†Getting started page†dev.ingonyama.com】 for full details about building and using ICICLE.\nL266: \nL267: Warning\nL268: \nL269: Ensure ICICLE libraries are installed correctly when building or installing a library/application that depends on ICICLE so that they can be located at runtime.\nL270: ### Rust\nL271: \nL272: In cargo.toml, specify the ICICLE libs to use:\nL273: \nL274:     [dependencies]\nL275:     icicle-runtime = { git = \"https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle.git\", branch=\"main\" }\nL276:     icicle-core = { git = \"https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle.git\", branch=\"main\" }\nL277:     icicle-babybear = { git = \"https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle.git\", branch=\"main\" }\nL278:     # add other ICICLE crates here if need additional fields/curves\nL279: \nL280: You can specify `branch=branch-name`, `tag=tag-name`, or `rev=commit-id`.\nL281: Build the Rust project:\nL282: \nL283:     cargo build --release\nL284: ### Go\nL285: \nL286: There are two ways to build from source in Go:\nL287: \nL288:   1. Clone the repo, update your go.mod to point to the local clone, and build ICICLE within the clone\nL289: \nL290:     git clone https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle.git\nL291: \nL292: Add ICICLE v3 to your go.mod file:\nL293: \nL294:     require github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3 v3.0.0\nL295: \nL296:     replace github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3 => ../path/to/cloned/icicle\nL297: Navigate to the cloned repo's golang bindings and build the library using the supplied 【122†build script】\nL298: \nL299:     cd icicle/wrappers/golang\nL300:     chmod +x build.sh\nL301:     ./build.sh -curve=bn254\nL302: \nL303:   2. Update your go.mod to include ICICLE as a dependency, navigate to the dependency in your GOMODCACHE and build ICICLE there\nL304: \nL305:     go get github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3\nL306:     cd $(go env GOMODCACHE)/github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3@<version>/wrappers/golang\nL307:     chmod +x build.sh\nL308:     ./build.sh -curve=bn254\nL309: Note\nL310: \nL311: To specify the field, use the flag -field=, where can be one of the following: babybear, stark252, m31, koalabear. To specify a curve, use the flag -curve=, where can be one of the following: bn254, bls12_377, bls12_381, bw6_761, grumpkin.\nL312: Once ICICLE has been built, you can add specific packages when you need them in your application:\nL313: \nL314:     import (\nL315:       runtime \"github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3/wrappers/golang/runtime\"\nL316:       core \"github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3/wrappers/golang/core\"\nL317:       bn254 \"github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3/wrappers/golang/curves/bn254\"\nL318:       bn254MSM \"github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3/wrappers/golang/curves/bn254/msm\"\nL319:     )\nL320: ### C++\nL321: \nL322: ICICLE can be built and tested in C++ using CMake. The build process is straightforward, but there are several flags you can use to customize the build for your needs.\nL323: \nL324: Clone the ICICLE repository:\nL325: \nL326:     git clone https://github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle.git\nL327:     cd icicle\nL328: \nL329: Configure the build:\nL330: \nL331:     mkdir -p build && rm -rf build/*\nL332:     cmake -S icicle -B build -DFIELD=babybear\nL333: \nL334: Note\nL335: To specify the field, use the flag -DFIELD=field, where field can be one of the following: babybear, stark252, m31, koalabear. To specify a curve, use the flag -DCURVE=curve, where curve can be one of the following: bn254, bls12_377, bls12_381, bw6_761, grumpkin.\nL336: \nL337: Build the project:\nL338: \nL339:     cmake --build build -j # -j is for multi-core compilation\nL340: \nL341: Link your application (or library) to ICICLE:\nL342: \nL343:     target_link_libraries(yourApp PRIVATE icicle_field_babybear icicle_device)\nL344: \nL345: Install (optional):\nL346: To install the libs, specify the install prefix `-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/install/dir/`. Then after building, use cmake to install the libraries:\nL347: \nL348:     cmake -S icicle -B build -DFIELD=babybear -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/path/to/install/dir/\nL349:     cmake --build build -j # build\nL350:     cmake --install build # install icicle to /path/to/install/dir/\nL351: \nL352: Run tests (optional):\nL353: \nL354: Caution\nL355: \nL356: Most tests assume a CUDA backend exists and will fail otherwise, if a CUDA device is not found.\nL357: Add `-DBUILD_TESTS=ON` to the cmake command, build and execute tests:\nL358: \nL359:     cmake -S icicle -B build -DFIELD=babybear -DBUILD_TESTS=ON\nL360:     cmake --build build -j\nL361:     cd build/tests\nL362:     ctest\nL363: \nL364: or choose the test-suite\nL365: \nL366:     ./build/tests/test_field_api # or another test suite\nL367:     # can specify tests using regex. For example for tests with ntt in the name:\nL368:     ./build/tests/test_field_api --gtest_filter=\"*ntt*\"\nL369: \nL370: Build Flags:\nL371: \nL372: You can customize your ICICLE build with the following flags:\nL373:   * `-DCPU_BACKEND=ON/OFF`: Enable or disable built-in CPU backend. `default=ON`.\nL374:   * `-DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=/install/dir`: Specify install directory. `default=/usr/local`.\nL375:   * `-DBUILD_TESTS=ON/OFF`: Enable or disable tests. `default=OFF`.\nL376:   * `-DBUILD_BENCHMARKS=ON/OFF`: Enable or disable benchmarks. `default=OFF`.\nL377: ## Install CUDA backend\nL378: \nL379: To install the CUDA backend\nL380: \nL381:   1. 【123†Download the release binaries】.\nL382:   2. Install it, by extracting the binaries to `/opt/` or any other custom install path.\nL383:   3. In your application, load the cuda backend and select a CUDA device.\nL384:   4. All subsequent API will now use the selected device.\nL385: Rust:\nL386: \nL387:     use icicle_runtime::{runtime, Device};\nL388: \nL389:     runtime::load_backend_from_env_or_default().unwrap();\nL390:     // or load programmatically\nL391:     runtime::load_backend(\"/path/to/backend/installdir\").unwrap();\nL392:     // Select CUDA device\nL393:     let device = Device::new(\"CUDA\", 1 /*gpu-id*/);\nL394:     icicle_runtime::set_device(&device).unwrap();\nL395: \nL396:     // Any call will now execute on GPU-1\nL397: Go:\nL398: \nL399:     import(\nL400:       \"github.com/ingonyama-zk/icicle/v3/wrappers/golang/runtime\"\nL401:     )\nL402: \nL403:     result := runtime.LoadBackendFromEnvOrDefault()\nL404:     // or load from custom install dir\nL405:     result := runtime.LoadBackend(\"/path/to/backend/installdir\", true)\nL406:     // Select CUDA device\nL407:     device := runtime.CreateDevice(\"CUDA\", 0) // or other\nL408:     result := runtime.SetDevice(device)\nL409: \nL410:     // Any call will now execute on GPU-0\nL411: C++:\nL412: \nL413:     #include \"icicle/runtime.h\"\nL414: \nL415:     // Load the installed backend\nL416:     eIcicleError result = icicle_load_backend_from_env_or_default();\nL417:     // or load it programmatically\nL418:     eIcicleError result = icicle_load_backend(\"/path/to/backend/installdir\", true);\nL419: \nL420:     // Select CUDA device\nL421:     icicle::Device device = {\"CUDA\", 0 /*gpu-id*/};\nL422:     eIcicleError result = icicle_set_device(device);\nL423: \nL424:     // Any call will now execute on GPU-0\nL425: Full details can be found in our 【124†getting started docs†dev.ingonyama.com】\nL426: ## Contributions\nL427: \nL428: Join our 【125†Discord Server†discord.gg】 and find us on the ICICLE channel. We will be happy to work together to support your use case, and talk features, bugs and design.\nL429: ### Development Contributions\nL430: \nL431: If you are changing code, please make sure to change your 【126†git hooks path†git-scm.com】 to the repo's 【127†hooks directory】 by running the following command:\nL432: \nL433:     git config core.hooksPath ./scripts/hooks\nL434: \nL435: In case `clang-format` is missing on your system, you can install it using the following command:\nL436: \nL437:     sudo apt install clang-format\nL438: \nL439: You will also need to install 【128†codespell】 to check for typos.\nL440: This will ensure our custom hooks are run and will make it easier to follow our coding guidelines.\nL441: ### Hall of Fame\nL442: \nL443:   * 【129†Robik】, for his ongoing support and mentorship\nL444:   * 【130†liuxiao】, for being a top notch bug smasher\n"
